---
import {getCollection} from 'astro:content';
import TimeSeries from '../../layouts/TimeSeries.astro';
const chapters=(await getCollection('timeseries')).sort((a,b)=>a.data.order-b.data.order);
---
<TimeSeries>
<div class="ea-shell"><section class="ea-hero"><div><p class="ea-kicker">A graduate course · First edition</p><h1>Time-Series Econometrics</h1><p class="ea-lead">Learn to defend a forecast through its economic purpose, mathematical argument, information set, and behavior on later outcomes.</p><p>By Ian Helfrich, PhD</p><div class="ea-actions"><a class="ea-button" href="/time-series/01-information/">Begin the book →</a><a class="ea-button secondary" href="/time-series/experiments/">Try an experiment</a></div><p class="ea-small">Fifteen lectures. Complete derivations and worked practice. Executable examples in R, Julia, and Python; choose one language for study.</p></div><figure class="ts-hero-figure"><img src="/time-series/figures/information-clock.svg" alt="A past decision admits released observations; a future observation is excluded until its release." width="720" height="430"/><figcaption>The information clock connects measurement, filtering, forecasting, and model revision.</figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="ea-band ea-split"><div><h2>An argument that changes with evidence</h2><p class="ea-lead">A model's derivation, implementation, audit, and revision belong to the same learning sequence.</p></div><div><p>Each lecture begins with an economic decision, develops the relevant mathematics, and tests it using matched code. Deliberate failure cases expose the assumptions. A repair must address the identified failure and survive a later comparison.</p><p>The applications connect financial risk with economic measurement, geographic exposure, and regional networks. The core remains time-series econometrics: stationary processes, integrated levels, dynamic regression, latent states, multivariate systems, cointegration, and conditional volatility.</p><p><a href="/time-series/methods/">Read the sources and teaching design</a></p></div></section>
<section class="ea-band" id="contents"><div class="ea-contents-head"><div><p class="ea-kicker">The complete course</p><h2>Fifteen lectures</h2></div><a href="/time-series/print/">Printable edition ↗</a></div><ol class="ea-chapters">{chapters.map(c=><li><a href={`/time-series/${c.id}/`}><span class="ea-number">{String(c.data.order).padStart(2,'0')}</span><div><strong>{c.data.title}</strong><p>{c.data.question}</p><small>{c.data.concepts.join(' · ')}</small></div><span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a></li>)}</ol></section>
<section class="ea-band"><h2>One decision across three projects</h2><div class="ts-project-list"><article><h3>Build a usable forecast</h3><p>Define the target and information rule, derive a model, and compare it with a benchmark on chronological targets.</p></article><article><h3>Audit a consequential claim</h3><p>Replicate a result, construct a counterexample, and repair a specific issue in timing, measurement, dynamics, or interpretation.</p></article><article><h3>Test a frozen revision</h3><p>Use later evidence to retain, revise, or retire the procedure, then evaluate the declared rule on continuation outcomes.</p></article></div><div class="ea-actions"><a class="ea-button" href="/time-series/projects/">Project specifications &amp; worked case →</a><a class="ea-button secondary" href="/time-series/practice/">Practice and worked answers</a></div></section>
<section class="ea-band ea-split"><div><h2>Reproduce the evidence</h2><p>Every lecture and project has independent source in three languages. Fixed fixtures and provider extracts make the examples runnable without API credentials.</p></div><div><p>The verification suite checks analytic quantities, future-information mutations, units, variance restrictions, numerical agreement, and matched forecast records. Its report includes failed-case tests and the outcome in which a seasonal benchmark narrowly beats the revised model.</p><a href="/time-series/code/">Download source, data, and verification results →</a></div></section>
</div>
</TimeSeries>
